1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,192
This is ECE 320, video 11. The topic is branches in the pipeline, and branch prediction.

2
00:00:08,642 --> 00:00:17,389
After this video, you can draw a pipeline diagram with a branch: the flush, the 2-cycle penalty, and a predicted-taken branch.

3
00:00:17,839 --> 00:00:21,423
You can calculate the CPI with a branch penalty.

4
00:00:21,873 --> 00:00:29,681
You can trace a one-bit B H T, a two-bit counter, and a global-history predictor, as in assignment 4.

5
00:00:30,131 --> 00:00:37,533
And you can fill a B H T and B T B table for a loop branch, as in the spring 2020 quiz.

6
00:00:37,983 --> 00:00:45,023
F must fetch a new instruction in every cycle. So in every cycle, F needs the next P C.

7
00:00:45,473 --> 00:00:53,708
A branch decides the next P C. Taken means that the condition is true. Then the next P C is the branch target.

8
00:00:54,158 --> 00:00:59,961
Not taken means that the condition is false. Then the next P C is P C plus 4.

9
00:01:00,411 --> 00:01:05,445
In F, only the P C is known. The instruction bits are not read yet.

10
00:01:05,895 --> 00:01:13,533
In D, the opcode and the offset are known. So D knows that it is a branch, and its target: P C plus offset.

11
00:01:13,983 --> 00:01:21,257
Only X knows the outcome: the branch condition, or the register value of a jump and link register.

12
00:01:21,707 --> 00:01:26,187
So the next P C is decided in X, but F needs it at once.

13
00:01:26,637 --> 00:01:32,013
By then, F and D hold 2 younger instructions. This is a control hazard.

14
00:01:32,463 --> 00:01:39,759
Option a: wait. F stalls until the branch reaches X, in cycle 3. There, the outcome is known.

15
00:01:40,209 --> 00:01:45,287
F stalls in cycles 2 and 3. So every branch loses 2 cycles.

16
00:01:45,737 --> 00:01:52,393
Option b: guess, and keep fetching. In cycle 1, F does not know yet that it fetched a branch.

17
00:01:52,843 --> 00:02:05,173
So the default guess is not taken: F fetches P C plus 4, then P C plus 8. If the guess is right, F did useful work in every cycle. No cycle is lost.

18
00:02:05,623 --> 00:02:12,685
Option c: the same guess, but the branch is taken. In cycle 3, X finds that the guess is wrong.

19
00:02:13,135 --> 00:02:18,916
The two wrong instructions, P C plus 4 and P C plus 8, become bubbles.

20
00:02:19,366 --> 00:02:26,299
In cycles 2 and 3, F fetched wrong instructions. So 2 cycles are lost: the same as waiting.

21
00:02:26,749 --> 00:02:35,027
This is speculation. Slide 10 gives its game: percent correct times gain, minus percent wrong times penalty.

22
00:02:35,477 --> 00:02:42,047
In this pipeline, a wrong guess costs 2 cycles, the same as waiting. So guessing never loses.

23
00:02:42,497 --> 00:02:52,289
The diagram is slide 4, drawn with the course rules. The guess is not taken. So in cycles 1 to 3, F fetches in program order.

24
00:02:52,739 --> 00:02:56,451
In cycle 4, the branch is in X, and it is taken.

25
00:02:56,901 --> 00:03:02,576
The green arrow forwards x 3 from add immediate, in M, to the branch in X.

26
00:03:03,026 --> 00:03:08,061
Store word is in D, and add is in F. Both are on the wrong path.

27
00:03:08,511 --> 00:03:12,137
In cycle 5, both become nops, shown as dashes.

28
00:03:12,587 --> 00:03:17,985
In the same cycle 5, F fetches the target. That is cycle X plus 1.

29
00:03:18,435 --> 00:03:26,499
F fetched store word in cycle 3, and add in cycle 4. Both did no work. So the penalty is 2 cycles.

30
00:03:26,949 --> 00:03:29,295
The target finishes in cycle 9.

31
00:03:29,745 --> 00:03:35,633
The flush is safe. Registers change only in W, and memory only in M.

32
00:03:36,083 --> 00:03:41,246
The wrong instructions reached only D. So they changed no permanent state.

33
00:03:41,696 --> 00:03:49,419
Slide 6 shows the hardware. The figure is a simplified form. F has the P C and the instruction memory.

34
00:03:49,869 --> 00:03:54,605
X has the branch compare unit, and an adder for P C plus immediate.

35
00:03:55,055 --> 00:03:59,684
The Branch signal comes from decode. It is true for a branch instruction.

36
00:04:00,134 --> 00:04:06,129
The Cond signal comes from the branch compare unit. It is true when the branch condition is true.

37
00:04:06,579 --> 00:04:09,309
Taken means Branch and Cond, both true.

38
00:04:09,759 --> 00:04:14,623
If the branch is taken, P C Sel sets the P C mux to the target.

39
00:04:15,073 --> 00:04:23,927
F Kill and D Kill set the kill bits. So the two younger instructions become nops: no register write, and no memory write.

40
00:04:24,377 --> 00:04:30,137
Slide 7: a conditional branch. Its condition is known in X, in cycle 3.

41
00:04:30,587 --> 00:04:34,789
If it is taken, the two instructions behind it become bubbles.

42
00:04:35,239 --> 00:04:44,071
Slide 8: jump and link register. Its target is a register value. That value is sure only in X, in cycle 3.

43
00:04:44,521 --> 00:04:46,505
So it has the same two bubbles.

44
00:04:46,955 --> 00:04:58,134
With the guess not taken, every taken branch loses 2 cycles. Every J A L R also loses 2 cycles, because it always changes the P C.

45
00:04:58,584 --> 00:05:07,373
Slide 9 gives a quick estimate. Take 100 instructions. 20 percent of them, so 20, are conditional branches.

46
00:05:07,823 --> 00:05:18,383
75 percent of the branches are taken: 20 times point 7 5 is 15. With the guess not taken, each taken branch is a wrong guess.

47
00:05:18,833 --> 00:05:24,209
Each wrong guess costs 2 cycles. 15 times 2 is 30 lost cycles.

48
00:05:24,659 --> 00:05:36,649
So the 100 instructions take 100 plus 30, which is 130 cycles. The CPI is 130 over 100, which is 1.3.

49
00:05:37,099 --> 00:05:44,672
The 30 lost cycles are 30 percent of the 100. So branches make the program 30 percent slower.

50
00:05:45,122 --> 00:05:49,453
A deeper pipeline has a larger penalty, so the loss is worse.

51
00:05:49,903 --> 00:05:56,345
To do better, the hardware must guess the outcome. F makes the guess when it selects the next P C.

52
00:05:56,795 --> 00:06:00,379
But the instruction bits are ready only at the end of F.

53
00:06:00,829 --> 00:06:03,539
So the predictor uses only the P C.

54
00:06:03,989 --> 00:06:08,981
It has two parts. A target buffer maps a P C to its taken target.

55
00:06:09,431 --> 00:06:13,868
A direction predictor maps a P C to taken or not taken.

56
00:06:14,318 --> 00:06:20,334
Slide 18 asks three questions. First: is it a branch. The predictor answers it.

57
00:06:20,784 --> 00:06:24,389
Second: is it taken. The direction predictor answers it.

58
00:06:24,839 --> 00:06:28,338
Third: where does it go. The target buffer answers it.

59
00:06:28,788 --> 00:06:33,524
The branch still resolves in X. A wrong guess is flushed, as before.

60
00:06:33,974 --> 00:06:40,715
To map a P C to a table entry, the hardware uses some P C bits as an index into an array.

61
00:06:41,165 --> 00:06:53,517
N entries need log 2 of N index bits. The index in the figure has 8 bits, bits 9 to 2. 8 bits give 2 to the 8, which is 256 entries.

62
00:06:53,967 --> 00:06:59,087
Bits 1 and 0 are always 0, because each instruction is 4 bytes.

63
00:06:59,537 --> 00:07:06,876
So the index uses the lowest bits above them. The low bits change most often, so they spread the branches best.

64
00:07:07,326 --> 00:07:14,046
Hex 1048 and hex 1448 differ only in bit 10.

65
00:07:14,496 --> 00:07:26,549
Their index bits, 9 to 2, are the same: 0 0 0 1 0 0 1 0, which is hex 12. So both use entry hex 12. This is aliasing.

66
00:07:26,999 --> 00:07:33,911
Aliasing is OK. It gives a nonsense guess, not a wrong result, because X still checks the branch.

67
00:07:34,361 --> 00:07:42,959
The simplest direction predictor is the branch history table, B H T. The index bits of the P C select one entry.

68
00:07:43,409 --> 00:07:49,254
Each entry is one bit: 0 for not taken, 1 for taken. There are no tags.

69
00:07:49,704 --> 00:08:01,160
It works because most branches are biased: they go one way 90 percent of the time or more. For example, a loop back edge is taken on every pass but the last.

70
00:08:01,610 --> 00:08:07,285
In F, the entry bit is the prediction: the branch goes the same way as last time.

71
00:08:07,735 --> 00:08:14,049
In X, the real outcome is known. The predictor writes the entry only if the guess was wrong.

72
00:08:14,499 --> 00:08:19,555
Slide 21 tests the B H T on an inner loop with 3 passes.

73
00:08:20,005 --> 00:08:26,789
Its branch goes taken, taken, taken, not taken, again and again. The entry starts at N.

74
00:08:27,239 --> 00:08:34,535
Time 1: the entry is N, so it predicts not taken. The branch is taken: wrong. The entry becomes T.

75
00:08:34,985 --> 00:08:40,489
Times 2 and 3: the entry is T, and the branch is taken. Both are correct.

76
00:08:40,939 --> 00:08:46,891
Time 4 is the exit: not taken. The entry says T: wrong. The entry becomes N.

77
00:08:47,341 --> 00:08:55,363
Time 5 starts the next run. The entry says N, but the branch is taken. Wrong again, and the entry becomes T.

78
00:08:55,813 --> 00:09:00,655
The pattern repeats. Count the wrong results: 6 misses in 12.

79
00:09:01,105 --> 00:09:06,887
So each run has two built-in misses: the exit, and the first pass of the next run.

80
00:09:07,337 --> 00:09:10,131
The predictor changes its mind too quickly.

81
00:09:10,581 --> 00:09:19,691
The fix is the two-bit saturating counter. It counts from 0 to 3. The states are N, small n, small t, and T.

82
00:09:20,141 --> 00:09:25,965
Taken adds 1: the arcs on top, labeled T. At T, taken stays at T.

83
00:09:26,415 --> 00:09:32,900
Not taken subtracts 1: the arcs below, labeled N. At N, not taken stays at N.

84
00:09:33,350 --> 00:09:38,278
States t and T predict taken. States N and n predict not taken.

85
00:09:38,728 --> 00:09:47,475
Same branch as before. Time 1: state N predicts not taken. The branch is taken: wrong. The counter moves to n.

86
00:09:47,925 --> 00:09:52,682
Time 2: n predicts not taken. Wrong again. The counter moves to t.

87
00:09:53,132 --> 00:09:57,633
Time 3: t predicts taken. Correct. The counter moves to T.

88
00:09:58,083 --> 00:10:06,019
Time 4 is the exit. T predicts taken: wrong. But the counter moves only to t, which still predicts taken.

89
00:10:06,469 --> 00:10:14,811
Time 5: t predicts taken. Correct. The counter must miss twice before it changes its mind. This is hysteresis.

90
00:10:15,261 --> 00:10:21,085
Times 6 to 12 follow the same rules. Count the wrong results: 5 misses.

91
00:10:21,535 --> 00:10:25,503
After warm-up, only one miss per run is left: the exit.

92
00:10:25,953 --> 00:10:33,249
Branches can be correlated. Slide 24 shows this code with three branches: A, B and C.

93
00:10:33,699 --> 00:10:42,573
If conditions A and B are true, then aa and bb are both 0. So aa equals bb, and condition C is false.

94
00:10:43,023 --> 00:10:50,789
A branch history shift register, the B H R, holds the last outcomes. A new outcome enters at the right.

95
00:10:51,239 --> 00:10:55,783
The P C bits select a row of the table, for example branch B.

96
00:10:56,233 --> 00:11:03,550
The B H R selects a column. The B H R in the figure is N T, so the column is N T.

97
00:11:04,000 --> 00:11:07,456
So each branch has one prediction per history pattern.

98
00:11:07,906 --> 00:11:21,794
Slide 26 uses the same T T T N branch, with 2 history bits. 2 bits give 4 patterns: N N, N T, T N and T T. So the branch has 4 one-bit entries.

99
00:11:22,244 --> 00:11:26,596
All entries start at N, and the B H R starts at N N.

100
00:11:27,046 --> 00:11:33,659
Time 1 reads entry N N, in the yellow frame. It predicts N, but the outcome is T: wrong.

101
00:11:34,109 --> 00:11:41,277
So entry N N becomes T. Then the outcome T shifts into the B H R, which becomes N T.

102
00:11:41,727 --> 00:11:47,509
Time 2 reads entry N T. It predicts N, but the outcome is T: wrong again.

103
00:11:47,959 --> 00:11:55,489
Time 3 reads entry T T. It predicts N, but the outcome is T: wrong. So T T becomes T.

104
00:11:55,939 --> 00:12:03,257
Time 4 reads T T again, now T. But this is the exit, N: wrong. So T T becomes N.

105
00:12:03,707 --> 00:12:07,163
In times 5 to 8, only time 6 is correct.

106
00:12:07,613 --> 00:12:16,999
Times 9 and 10 are also correct. Count the wrong results: 9 misses in 12. That is worse than the 2-bit counter, with 5.

107
00:12:17,449 --> 00:12:26,857
History T T comes before T at time 3, and before N at time 4. One entry cannot give both. The history is too short.

108
00:12:27,307 --> 00:12:36,694
Slide 27 uses 3 history bits. 3 bits give 2 to the 3, which is 8 patterns. So the branch has 8 entries.

109
00:12:37,144 --> 00:12:43,309
In times 1 to 6, each history pattern is new. So each time reads a fresh entry.

110
00:12:43,759 --> 00:12:48,751
A fresh entry says N. So the predictor misses 5 times while it learns.

111
00:12:49,201 --> 00:12:54,684
Time 4 is correct only because the exit is N, the same as the fresh entry.

112
00:12:55,134 --> 00:12:58,185
From time 7, every prediction is correct.

113
00:12:58,635 --> 00:13:05,184
Each pattern of the loop, N T T, T T T, T T N and T N T, has its own entry.

114
00:13:05,634 --> 00:13:09,581
History T T T is always followed by the exit, N.

115
00:13:10,031 --> 00:13:13,529
History N T T is always followed by taken.

116
00:13:13,979 --> 00:13:16,774
The history must be long enough to see the pattern.

117
00:13:17,224 --> 00:13:24,136
A hybrid predictor combines two schemes. A simple B H T predicts the independent branches.

118
00:13:24,586 --> 00:13:30,495
A correlated B H T, with global history, predicts the branches that need history.

119
00:13:30,945 --> 00:13:34,017
A chooser assigns each branch to one of them.

120
00:13:34,467 --> 00:13:40,803
Branches start in the simple B H T. They move to the correlated one after too many misses.

121
00:13:41,253 --> 00:13:45,520
The slides give 90 to 97 percent correct predictions.

122
00:13:45,970 --> 00:13:50,429
Option a: predict in D. In D, the branch target is known.

123
00:13:50,879 --> 00:13:54,911
But in cycle 2, F has already fetched P C plus 4.

124
00:13:55,361 --> 00:14:03,723
A taken guess fetches the target in cycle 3. So even a correct taken guess costs 1 cycle. This is a mis-fetch.

125
00:14:04,173 --> 00:14:13,901
Option b: predict in F. A correct taken guess fetches the target in cycle 2, right after the branch. 0 cycles are lost.

126
00:14:14,351 --> 00:14:24,954
But in F, only the P C is known. So the predictor must also tell if it is a branch, and where it goes. That is the job of the branch target buffer.

127
00:14:25,404 --> 00:14:34,983
The branch target buffer, B T B, records past targets. If a branch went to a target last time, F fetches that target after it.

128
00:14:35,433 --> 00:14:46,163
Every instruction reads the B T B in F. Each entry has a tag: the full P C of a branch. A comparator checks the tag against the P C.

129
00:14:46,613 --> 00:14:52,971
A tag match is a hit: the P C is a known branch. Then the mux selects the stored target.

130
00:14:53,421 --> 00:14:56,621
No match: the mux selects P C plus 4.

131
00:14:57,071 --> 00:15:04,985
Every taken branch writes its entry in X. The tag gets the P C, and the target gets the target P C.

132
00:15:05,435 --> 00:15:16,251
An example. The index is P C bits 9 to 2. Hex 1 C has the index bits 0 0 0 0 0 1 1 1, which is 7.

133
00:15:16,701 --> 00:15:24,232
Hex 4 1 C has the same index bits, so it also reads entry 7. Only bit 10 is different.

134
00:15:24,682 --> 00:15:31,402
Entry 7 holds the tag hex 1 C. Hex 4 1 C does not match, so there is no hit.

135
00:15:31,852 --> 00:15:40,023
So F fetches P C plus 4: hex 4 1 C plus 4 is hex 4 2 0. The tag stops aliasing.

136
00:15:40,473 --> 00:15:48,921
A B T B works because most branches use direct targets. The target is P C plus an offset, in the instruction.

137
00:15:49,371 --> 00:15:55,835
So the taken target is the same every time. The B T B stores it once: a good guess.

138
00:15:56,285 --> 00:16:03,666
Indirect targets are in a register, so they can change. The B T B holds only the last target.

139
00:16:04,116 --> 00:16:13,759
J A L R is common: every procedure return is a J A L R. One function returns to a different place for each caller.

140
00:16:14,209 --> 00:16:21,142
The return address stack, R A S, predicts returns. A call, J A L, pushes P C plus 4.

141
00:16:21,592 --> 00:16:26,925
A return pops the top of the stack. The popped value is the predicted target.

142
00:16:27,375 --> 00:16:34,415
Call 1 is at hex 100. It pushes hex 100 plus 4, which is hex 104.

143
00:16:34,865 --> 00:16:39,921
Return 1 pops hex 104. That is the correct return address.

144
00:16:40,371 --> 00:16:48,585
The B T B has no entry for this return yet. So it guesses P C plus 4, hex 510: wrong.

145
00:16:49,035 --> 00:16:56,053
Call 2 is at hex 300. It pushes hex 300 plus 4, which is hex 304.

146
00:16:56,503 --> 00:16:59,938
Return 2 pops hex 304: correct.

147
00:17:00,388 --> 00:17:07,023
The B T B holds the last target of this return, hex 104. That is wrong now.

148
00:17:07,473 --> 00:17:21,211
F must know that an instruction is a return before decode, because a pop changes the stack. Pre-decode bits, stored with each instruction in the instruction memory, mark calls and returns.

149
00:17:21,661 --> 00:17:31,603
Slide 35 puts it all together. Start with the base. In F, the P C goes to the plus 4 adder, and the mux gives the next P C.

150
00:17:32,053 --> 00:17:38,069
In parallel, the P C reads the B T B. On a tag hit, it gives the stored target.

151
00:17:38,519 --> 00:17:43,809
The P C also reads the B H T. It predicts taken or not taken.

152
00:17:44,259 --> 00:17:48,441
The R A S gives the return P C: the top of the stack.

153
00:17:48,891 --> 00:17:54,139
The pre-decode bits in the instruction memory tell if the instruction is a return.

154
00:17:54,589 --> 00:17:57,469
The mux picks the R A S top for a return.

155
00:17:57,919 --> 00:18:02,249
It picks the B T B target on a tag hit with a taken prediction.

156
00:18:02,699 --> 00:18:05,195
Otherwise, it picks P C plus 4.

157
00:18:05,645 --> 00:18:09,955
A correct guess costs 0 cycles, even for a taken branch.

158
00:18:10,405 --> 00:18:22,714
With 75 percent correct: per 100 instructions, 5 of the 20 branches are wrong. 5 times 2 is 10 lost cycles, so the CPI is 1.1.

159
00:18:23,164 --> 00:18:37,521
With 95 percent correct: 1 of the 20 branches is wrong. 1 times 2 is 2 lost cycles, so the CPI is 1.02. Real penalties are often 10 cycles or more.

160
00:18:37,971 --> 00:18:45,737
Predication removes the branch. In conventional code, the branch is mis-predicted. It flushes both sub and add.

161
00:18:46,187 --> 00:18:51,392
But add is on both paths. It is fetched again at targ, so flushing it is waste.

162
00:18:51,842 --> 00:18:59,181
With predication, there is no branch. The set-predicate instruction writes the predicate register, p 3.

163
00:18:59,631 --> 00:19:06,180
p 3 controls sub. If p 3 is false, sub is annulled in place: it does not write.

164
00:19:06,630 --> 00:19:09,361
Add is kept. It runs with no bubble.

165
00:19:09,811 --> 00:19:14,611
The benefit: no mis-predictions, and fewer branches in the predictor tables.

166
00:19:15,061 --> 00:19:23,914
The cost: annulled instructions still use pipeline slots. With accurate predictors, predication may not help, and may even hurt.

167
00:19:24,364 --> 00:19:28,759
A practice problem with the slide numbers. Pause the video and try it.

168
00:19:29,209 --> 00:19:35,865
Count per 100 instructions. 20 percent are branches, so each row shows 20 branches.

169
00:19:36,315 --> 00:19:49,861
a. Stall on every branch: all 20 branches lose 2 cycles. 20 times 2 is 40 lost cycles. So the CPI is 100 plus 40, over 100: 1.4.

170
00:19:50,311 --> 00:19:59,207
b. Predict not taken: the 15 taken branches are wrong. 15 times 2 is 30, so the CPI is 1.3.

171
00:19:59,657 --> 00:20:10,324
c. 75 percent correct means 25 percent wrong: 5 of the 20 branches. 5 times 2 is 10, so the CPI is 1.1.

172
00:20:10,774 --> 00:20:21,057
d. 95 percent correct means 5 percent wrong: 1 of the 20 branches. 1 times 2 is 2, so the CPI is 1.02.

173
00:20:21,507 --> 00:20:29,827
e. The same 1 wrong branch, but with a 10-cycle penalty. 1 times 10 is 10, so the CPI is 1.1 again.

174
00:20:30,277 --> 00:20:35,290
With a deep pipeline, the penalty is large. So a good predictor matters more.

175
00:20:35,740 --> 00:20:43,847
Assignment 4, question 1. Pause the video and try each part. The question gives its own 2-bit state machine.

176
00:20:44,297 --> 00:20:51,657
States 0 0 and 0 1 predict taken. States 1 0 and 1 1 predict not taken.

177
00:20:52,107 --> 00:21:03,456
It is not the slide counter. From 1 1, taken goes to 0 0. From 0 1, not taken goes to 1 0. Always use the machine of the question.

178
00:21:03,906 --> 00:21:12,930
b1 is branch if equal to zero, on x4. It skips the add immediate of x2 when the element x4 is 0.

179
00:21:13,380 --> 00:21:19,972
b2 is branch if not equal to zero, on x1. It loops while x1 is not 0.

180
00:21:20,422 --> 00:21:30,747
x1 starts at n, which is 8. The array alternates 1 and 0. All entries start at 1 0. b1 and b2 do not alias.

181
00:21:31,197 --> 00:21:41,949
Part a. The 8 elements are 1, 0, 1, 0, 1, 0, 1, 0. x2 counts the non-zero ones: 4. So x2 is 4.

182
00:21:42,399 --> 00:21:47,370
b1 is taken when x4 is 0: not taken, taken, and so on.

183
00:21:47,820 --> 00:21:57,463
Add immediate counts x1 down from 8 to 0. b2 is taken while x1 is not 0: 7 times, then not taken.

184
00:21:57,913 --> 00:22:07,001
Part b uses one entry per branch, with no history. Pause the video and try it. The yellow frame marks the entry that is read.

185
00:22:07,451 --> 00:22:16,880
Row 1: b1 reads 1 0. It predicts not taken, and it is not taken: correct. On N, 1 0 stays at 1 0.

186
00:22:17,330 --> 00:22:25,949
Row 2: b2 reads 1 0 and predicts not taken. But it is taken: a miss. On T, b2 moves to 1 1.

187
00:22:26,399 --> 00:22:34,633
Row 3: b1 reads 1 0. The element is 0, so b1 is taken: a miss. b1 moves to 1 1.

188
00:22:35,083 --> 00:22:43,617
Row 4: b2 reads 1 1, predicts not taken, and is taken: a miss. On T, 1 1 moves to 0 0.

189
00:22:44,067 --> 00:22:52,429
Row 5: b1 reads 1 1, predicts not taken, and is not taken: correct. b1 moves back to 1 0.

190
00:22:52,879 --> 00:23:00,047
Row 6: b2 reads 0 0 and predicts taken: correct. b2 stays at 0 0.

191
00:23:00,497 --> 00:23:08,092
b1 moves between 1 0 and 1 1. Both predict not taken, so every taken b1 misses.

192
00:23:08,542 --> 00:23:12,553
b2 stays at 0 0, and it is correct each time.

193
00:23:13,003 --> 00:23:20,469
Rows 11 to 15 repeat this pattern. The final b2, in row 16, is not taken: a miss.

194
00:23:20,919 --> 00:23:25,591
Count the misses: 7. 4 are from b1, and 3 are from b2.

195
00:23:26,041 --> 00:23:35,364
Part c adds one global history bit: the outcome of the last branch, b1 or b2. 1 means taken. It starts at 1.

196
00:23:35,814 --> 00:23:42,577
The history bit selects the entry: set 0 or set 1. Each branch has its own two entries.

197
00:23:43,027 --> 00:23:52,093
Before each b1, the last branch is b2, and b2 is taken. So the history is 1, and b1 always reads set 1.

198
00:23:52,543 --> 00:23:57,279
That is the same as no history. b1 still misses each taken branch.

199
00:23:57,729 --> 00:24:07,372
b2 follows b1. b1 alternates not taken and taken, so b2 sees the history 0, then 1, again and again.

200
00:24:07,822 --> 00:24:13,475
So b2 trains both sets. Each set misses twice before it reaches 0 0.

201
00:24:13,925 --> 00:24:19,088
From row 10, b2 reads 0 0 in both sets, and it is correct.

202
00:24:19,538 --> 00:24:23,591
The final b2, in row 16, is not taken: a miss.

203
00:24:24,041 --> 00:24:31,380
Count the misses: 9. 4 are from b1, and 5 are from b2. One history bit made it worse.

204
00:24:31,830 --> 00:24:40,427
Part d uses 2 history bits. In this question, the newest outcome enters on the left. The history starts at 1 1.

205
00:24:40,877 --> 00:24:47,491
2 bits give 4 patterns. So each branch has 4 entries, S 0 0 to S 1 1.

206
00:24:47,941 --> 00:24:57,498
b1 sees the history 1 1 before each not taken, and 1 0 before each taken. So b1 uses two different entries.

207
00:24:57,948 --> 00:25:07,569
Entry S 1 0 sees only taken outcomes. It moves from 1 0 to 1 1, then to 0 0, which predicts taken.

208
00:25:08,019 --> 00:25:15,550
Entry S 1 1 sees only not taken outcomes. It stays at 1 0, which predicts not taken.

209
00:25:16,000 --> 00:25:22,784
So from row 9, b1 is always correct. b1 misses only twice, in rows 3 and 7.

210
00:25:23,234 --> 00:25:30,935
b2 misses 4 times while its entries S 0 1 and S 1 1 train, and once at the final exit.

211
00:25:31,385 --> 00:25:39,172
Total: 7 mispredicts, 2 from b1 and 5 from b2. After warm-up, only the final b2 misses.

212
00:25:39,622 --> 00:25:48,881
Compare the results. Each block is one mispredict. No history has 7, 1 history bit has 9, and 2 history bits have 7.

213
00:25:49,331 --> 00:25:52,616
All three miss the last b2: the loop exit.

214
00:25:53,066 --> 00:25:58,463
Part e. More history bits mean more entries to train, so a longer start-up.

215
00:25:58,913 --> 00:26:04,716
One history bit is too short-sighted to help b1: b1 still misses 4 times.

216
00:26:05,166 --> 00:26:13,166
For large n, 2 bits are best. They capture the alternating b1, which then misses only twice, at the start.

217
00:26:13,616 --> 00:26:26,160
Part f. With random input, no scheme predicts b1, but all learn that b2 is taken. This does not depend on n. For large n, the start-up costs become small.

218
00:26:26,610 --> 00:26:35,591
Part g. History helps when there is a pattern, and the history is long enough to see it. Otherwise it hurts, because training takes longer.

219
00:26:36,041 --> 00:26:47,049
Now the spring 2020 quiz, question 2 part A. Pause the video and try it. There is bypassing, but not into D, and not into the branch compare unit.

220
00:26:47,499 --> 00:26:52,449
The branch, blt, is predicted taken, but it resolves not taken.

221
00:26:52,899 --> 00:26:59,619
Add needs t 0 from the load. That is a load-use stall: add waits in D in cycle 3.

222
00:27:00,069 --> 00:27:06,703
In cycle 5, the load is in W. W X forwarding gives t 0 to add in X.

223
00:27:07,153 --> 00:27:09,543
The branch is fetched in cycle 5.

224
00:27:09,993 --> 00:27:18,441
The branch needs a 0 from add immediate, with no forwarding. Add immediate writes a 0 in W, in cycle 8.

225
00:27:18,891 --> 00:27:26,293
D reads only values that were written in earlier cycles. So the branch reads a 0 in D in cycle 9.

226
00:27:26,743 --> 00:27:32,269
The predictor says taken. So from cycle 6, F fetches the target, hex 10.

227
00:27:32,719 --> 00:27:37,817
In cycle 10, the branch resolves in X: not taken. The guess was wrong.

228
00:27:38,267 --> 00:27:42,129
The wrong load and add become dashes, from cycle 11.

229
00:27:42,579 --> 00:27:46,333
In cycle 11, F fetches the nop at hex 20.

230
00:27:46,783 --> 00:27:49,045
The nop finishes in cycle 15.

231
00:27:49,495 --> 00:27:52,417
But the quiz table has only 14 columns.

232
00:27:52,867 --> 00:28:03,043
It fits if the register file writes, then reads, in the same cycle. Then the branch reads a 0 in D in cycle 8, the cycle of the write.

233
00:28:03,493 --> 00:28:11,707
Every later cell moves one cycle earlier, so the nop finishes in cycle 14. State your assumption in the exam.

234
00:28:12,157 --> 00:28:20,178
Part B adds a 2-bit counter B H T and a B T B. Pause the video and fill the table for blt.

235
00:28:20,628 --> 00:28:23,743
States 1 0 and 1 1 predict taken.

236
00:28:24,193 --> 00:28:29,142
a 0 starts at hex 1000, and a 1 is hex 1010.

237
00:28:29,592 --> 00:28:41,965
Add immediate adds 4 to a 0 before each blt. So blt sees hex 1004, 1008 and 100 C. Each is less than a 1: taken.

238
00:28:42,415 --> 00:28:50,223
Then blt sees hex 1010, which equals a 1: not taken. So blt runs 4 times.

239
00:28:50,673 --> 00:28:59,399
Run 1 is given. The entry 0 1 predicts not taken, but blt is taken. So the entry moves to 1 0.

240
00:28:59,849 --> 00:29:06,035
A taken branch writes the B T B. So the B T B now holds the target, hex 10.

241
00:29:06,485 --> 00:29:14,763
Run 2: the entry is 1 0. It predicts taken, with the target hex 10. Correct, so the entry moves to 1 1.

242
00:29:15,213 --> 00:29:20,375
Run 3: 1 1 predicts taken. Correct, and the entry stays at 1 1.

243
00:29:20,825 --> 00:29:31,791
Run 4: 1 1 predicts taken, but blt is not taken. The entry moves to 1 0. A not-taken branch does not write the B T B.

244
00:29:32,241 --> 00:29:38,555
The last row is the state after the loop: entry 1 0, predict taken, target hex 10.

245
00:29:39,005 --> 00:29:44,019
Rows 5 and 6 stay blank, because blt runs only 4 times.

246
00:29:44,469 --> 00:29:52,661
Recap. One. Branches and J A L R resolve in X. By then, F and D hold two younger instructions.

247
00:29:53,111 --> 00:30:00,279
Two. A wrong guess flushes them, and fetches the right P C in the next cycle. The penalty is 2 cycles.

248
00:30:00,729 --> 00:30:07,214
Three. CPI is 1, plus the branch fraction, times the wrong fraction, times the penalty.

249
00:30:07,664 --> 00:30:14,704
Four. Index tables with the P C bits above bits 1 and 0. Aliasing only spoils a guess.

250
00:30:15,154 --> 00:30:23,005
Five. A one-bit B H T misses twice per loop run, a two-bit counter once. Use the machine of the question.

251
00:30:23,455 --> 00:30:30,025
Six. A history predictor keeps one entry per P C and pattern. The history must be long enough.

252
00:30:30,475 --> 00:30:38,241
Seven. In F, the B T B gives the target, the B H T the direction, and the R A S the return address.

253
00:30:38,691 --> 00:30:45,112
Eight. In the exam, state your register file assumption. That is the end of video 11.
